{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon EKS and Amazon EKS Distro now supports Kubernetes version 1.35 - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (EKS) and Amazon EKS Distro now support Kubernetes version 1.35. Users can create new EKS clusters using version 1.35 and upgrade existing clusters through the EKS console, eksctl command line interface, or infrastructure-as-code tools. Kubernetes version 1.35 introduces several new features such as In-Place Pod Resource Updates, PreferSameNode Traffic Distribution, Node Topology Labels via Downward API, and Image Volumes. These improvements allow CPU and memory adjustments without Pod restarts, prioritize local endpoints for reduced latency, enable Pods to access region and zone information, and deliver data artifacts using OCI container images, respectively. EKS supports Kubernetes version 1.35 in all regions where EKS is available, including AWS GovCloud (US) Regions. EKS Distro builds of Kubernetes version 1.35 are available through ECR Public Gallery and GitHub. Users can check for potential issues impacting Kubernetes cluster upgrades using EKS cluster insights","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/01/amazon-eks-distro-kubernetes-version-1-35/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"aws-containers","skill_description":"Builds, deploys, debugs, and optimizes containerized workloads on Amazon EKS, ECS, Fargate, ECR, and Elastic Beanstalk. Covers EKS operations and add-ons; ECS task definitions, services, Exec, Express Mode, Managed Instances, and Action Logs; ECR repositories and lifecycle policies; and Elastic Beanstalk platforms plus Beanstalk Cluster Mode for running containerized applications on service-managed EKS compute. Use for container deployment, service selection, configuration, scaling, health, troubleshooting, image workflows, or deciding between Beanstalk-managed Cluster Mode and direct EKS. Should be used instead of relying on internal knowledge for these services.\n\nServices: ecs, ecr, eks, apprunner, beanstalk\nTasks: deploy, debug, optimize\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: containers, compute, kubernetes\n*","skill_name":"aws-containers"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"amazon-keyspaces","skill_description":"Provides authoritative compatibility checks, pricing estimates, connection troubleshooting, pre-warming guidance, and infrastructure mutations for Amazon Keyspaces (for Apache Cassandra). Covers LWT/batch operations, secondary indexes, materialized views, capacity modes, TTL, PITR, CDC, auto-scaling, multi-region keyspaces, UDTs, nodetool diagnostics parsing, SQL-to-Cassandra migration, and Cassandra-to-Keyspaces migration scenarios. Agents frequently produce incomplete or incorrect answers about Keyspaces feature support without this skill loaded.\n\nServices: keyspaces, cassandra\nTasks: cost-estimation, migration-assessment, compatibility-check, data-modeling, create, modify\nPersona: developer, devops, architect, dba\nWorkload: nosql, wide-column-store, cassandra-migration\n*","skill_name":"amazon-keyspaces"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"Application Performance Monitoring Enabled by Default in CloudWatch Observability EKS Add-on - AWS","context":"Amazon CloudWatch Observability EKS add-on version 5.0.0 now enables Application Signals, Amazon's application performance monitoring (APM) capability, by default for all new installations and upgrades of Elastic Kubernetes Service (EKS). This eliminates the need for manual opt-in steps. The add-on extends native Kubernetes observability by integrating Enhanced Container Insights, Container Logs, and Application Signals directly into EKS clusters. Application Signals automatically instruments services to collect traces, metrics, and logs for a unified, application-centric view. DevOps engineers and developers can now benefit from automatic service instrumentation and pre-built dashboards that surface application performance metrics, enabling quick identification and resolution of issues. Application Signals is available in all commercial AWS regions where Amazon CloudWatch Application Signals is available. To get started, customers can upgrade to version 5.0.0 of the add-on and refer to the Amazon CloudWatch Application Signals documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/02/application-performance-monitoring-cloudwatch-eks/"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"Amazon EKS Node Monitoring Agent is now open source - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (Amazon EKS) has made the Node Monitoring Agent open source, allowing users to access its source code on GitHub for customization and development contributions. The agent simplifies monitoring and repairing degraded nodes in Kubernetes clusters by automatically publishing node-level system, storage, networking, and accelerator issues as node conditions. It is included in Amazon EKS Auto Mode and available as an add-on in all AWS regions where Amazon EKS is available. The agent's open-source availability provides users with insight into its implementation and the ability to tailor it to their specific requirements. For more information on the Amazon EKS Node Monitoring Agent and node repair, visit the Amazon EKS documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/02/amazon-eks-node-monitoring-agent-open-source/"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Look up AWS docs on EKS Cluster Insights check categories"}
